{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-123050" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/f77ec60f3208cd221570e30ed8f4c1684607dc54567435071dd34ccf76ea5227.png)\n\nConsider the partition of the rectangular region $R$ shown above and the positive function $f(x,y)$ defined on $R.$ Which of the following gives an expression for the upper Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to this partition.\n\n*Hint: ${M}_{ij}$ denotes the maximum value of $f$ on the subregion ${R}_{ij}$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/state.json" "file_sha256" "610429aa6dd5fcf5908c798c40fcb2ff3748ed1a5c18b759c6a6f7829018963a"} {"question" "q-123050" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To calculate the upper Riemann sum $U$ with respect to the given partition, we compute the sum of the volumes of $2$ rectangular solids, one for each ${R}_{ij}.$\n\nThe volume of the rectangular solid corresponding to each subregion ${R}_{ij}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{M}_{ij}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{ij}).\n$$\n\nwhere ${M}_{ij}$ denotes the maximum value of $f$ on the subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nSo in our case, we have\n\n$$\nU={M}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{M}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12}).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}({R}_{11})=1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}({R}_{12})=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & ={M}_{11}+3\\cdot {M}_{12} \\\\  & ={M}_{11}+3{M}_{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/state.json" "file_sha256" "610429aa6dd5fcf5908c798c40fcb2ff3748ed1a5c18b759c6a6f7829018963a"} {"question" "q-123050" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "30305aa75b06bac37b4cc4676cd685805b15f81588548b9edcb6adcf66fef700"} {"question" "q-123050" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/state.json" "file_sha256" "610429aa6dd5fcf5908c798c40fcb2ff3748ed1a5c18b759c6a6f7829018963a"} {"question" "q-123050" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "3{M}_{11}+{M}_{12}"] ["math" "3{M}_{11}\\cdot {M}_{12}"] ["math" "{M}_{11}+3{M}_{12}"] ["math" "{M}_{11}+{M}_{12}"] ["math" "{M}_{11}\\cdot {M}_{12}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/state.json" "file_sha256" "610429aa6dd5fcf5908c798c40fcb2ff3748ed1a5c18b759c6a6f7829018963a"} {"question" "q-123050" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{M}_{11}+3{M}_{12}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/state.json" "file_sha256" "610429aa6dd5fcf5908c798c40fcb2ff3748ed1a5c18b759c6a6f7829018963a"} {"question" "q-123051" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/c11167f3e67f712b894cc56aef96897e5a75b1a3e0d1e3ca214f2fd48a70febb.png)\n\nConsider the partition of the rectangular region $R$ shown above and the positive function $f(x,y)$ defined on $R.$ Which of the following gives an expression for the upper Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to this partition.\n\n*Hint: ${M}_{ij}$ denotes the maximum value of $f$ on the subregion ${R}_{ij}$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/state.json" "file_sha256" "610429aa6dd5fcf5908c798c40fcb2ff3748ed1a5c18b759c6a6f7829018963a"} {"question" "q-123051" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To calculate the upper Riemann sum $U$ with respect to the given partition, we compute the sum of the volumes of $3$ rectangular solids, one for each ${R}_{ij}.$\n\nThe volume of the rectangular solid corresponding to each subregion ${R}_{ij}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{M}_{ij}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{ij}).\n$$\n\nwhere ${M}_{ij}$ denotes the maximum value of $f$ on the subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nSo in our case, we have\n\n$$\nU={M}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{M}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{M}_{13}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{13}).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}({R}_{11})=\\text{Area}({R}_{13})=1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}({R}_{12})=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & ={M}_{11}+2\\cdot {M}_{12}+{M}_{13} \\\\  & ={M}_{11}+2{M}_{12}+{M}_{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/state.json" "file_sha256" "610429aa6dd5fcf5908c798c40fcb2ff3748ed1a5c18b759c6a6f7829018963a"} {"question" "q-123051" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "30305aa75b06bac37b4cc4676cd685805b15f81588548b9edcb6adcf66fef700"} {"question" "q-123051" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/state.json" "file_sha256" "610429aa6dd5fcf5908c798c40fcb2ff3748ed1a5c18b759c6a6f7829018963a"} {"question" "q-123051" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2{M}_{11}+{M}_{12}+{M}_{13}"] ["math" "2{M}_{11}\\cdot {M}_{12}\\cdot {M}_{13}"] ["math" "{M}_{11}+2{M}_{12}+{M}_{13}"] ["math" "{M}_{11}+{M}_{12}+2{M}_{13}"] ["math" "{M}_{11}+{M}_{12}+{M}_{13}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/state.json" "file_sha256" "610429aa6dd5fcf5908c798c40fcb2ff3748ed1a5c18b759c6a6f7829018963a"} {"question" "q-123051" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{M}_{11}+2{M}_{12}+{M}_{13}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/state.json" "file_sha256" "610429aa6dd5fcf5908c798c40fcb2ff3748ed1a5c18b759c6a6f7829018963a"} {"question" "q-123049" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/76654da8bebc4a156a55bcb6cc7411f4977dfa14634fbceb6e1a7f8e32b2d5b0.png)\n\nConsider the partition of the rectangular region $R$ shown above and the positive function $f(x,y)$ defined on $R.$ Which of the following gives an expression for the upper Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to this partition.\n\n*Hint: ${M}_{ij}$ denotes the maximum value of $f$ on the subregion ${R}_{ij}$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/state.json" "file_sha256" "610429aa6dd5fcf5908c798c40fcb2ff3748ed1a5c18b759c6a6f7829018963a"} {"question" "q-123049" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To calculate the upper Riemann sum $U$ with respect to the given partition, we compute the sum of the volumes of $3$ rectangular solids, one for each ${R}_{ij}.$\n\nThe volume of the rectangular solid corresponding to each subregion ${R}_{ij}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{M}_{ij}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{ij}).\n$$\n\nwhere ${M}_{ij}$ denotes the maximum value of $f$ on the subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nSo in our case, we have\n\n$$\nU={M}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{M}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{M}_{31}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{31}).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}({R}_{11})=6, \\\\ \\text{Area}({R}_{21})=2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}({R}_{31})=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =6\\cdot {M}_{11}+2\\cdot {M}_{21}+4\\cdot {M}_{31} \\\\  & =6{M}_{11}+2{M}_{21}+4{M}_{31}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/state.json" "file_sha256" "610429aa6dd5fcf5908c798c40fcb2ff3748ed1a5c18b759c6a6f7829018963a"} {"question" "q-123049" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "30305aa75b06bac37b4cc4676cd685805b15f81588548b9edcb6adcf66fef700"} {"question" "q-123049" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/state.json" "file_sha256" "610429aa6dd5fcf5908c798c40fcb2ff3748ed1a5c18b759c6a6f7829018963a"} {"question" "q-123049" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "3{M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+2{M}_{31}"] ["math" "48{M}_{11}\\cdot {M}_{21}\\cdot {M}_{31}"] ["math" "6{M}_{11}+2{M}_{21}+4{M}_{31}"] ["math" "{M}_{11}+3{M}_{21}+2{M}_{31}"] ["math" "{M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+{M}_{31}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/state.json" "file_sha256" "610429aa6dd5fcf5908c798c40fcb2ff3748ed1a5c18b759c6a6f7829018963a"} {"question" "q-123049" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "6{M}_{11}+2{M}_{21}+4{M}_{31}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/state.json" "file_sha256" "610429aa6dd5fcf5908c798c40fcb2ff3748ed1a5c18b759c6a6f7829018963a"} {"question" "q-122847" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/c28d3a6098da6c8be022974e96b5242e3bed58ef1347d92571b49c7b521a3b0d.png)\n\nConsider the partition of the rectangular region $R$ shown above and the positive function $f(x,y)$ defined on $R.$ Which of the following gives an expression for the upper Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to this partition.\n\n*Hint: ${M}_{ij}$ denotes the maximum value of $f$ on the subregion ${R}_{ij}$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/state.json" "file_sha256" "610429aa6dd5fcf5908c798c40fcb2ff3748ed1a5c18b759c6a6f7829018963a"} {"question" "q-122847" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To calculate the upper Riemann sum $U$ with respect to the given partition, we compute the sum of the volumes of $4$ rectangular solids, one for each ${R}_{ij}.$\n\nThe volume of the rectangular solid corresponding to each subregion ${R}_{ij}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{M}_{ij}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{ij}).\n$$\n\nwhere ${M}_{ij}$ denotes the maximum value of $f$ on the subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nSo in our case, we have\n\n$$\nU={M}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{M}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{M}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{M}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22}).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}({R}_{11})=\\text{Area}({R}_{12})=2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}({R}_{21})=\\text{Area}({R}_{22})=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =2\\cdot {M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+2\\cdot {M}_{12}+{M}_{22} \\\\  & =2{M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+2{M}_{12}+{M}_{22}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/state.json" "file_sha256" "610429aa6dd5fcf5908c798c40fcb2ff3748ed1a5c18b759c6a6f7829018963a"} {"question" "q-122847" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "30305aa75b06bac37b4cc4676cd685805b15f81588548b9edcb6adcf66fef700"} {"question" "q-122847" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/state.json" "file_sha256" "610429aa6dd5fcf5908c798c40fcb2ff3748ed1a5c18b759c6a6f7829018963a"} {"question" "q-122847" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2({M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+{M}_{12}+{M}_{22})"] ["math" "2{M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+2{M}_{12}+{M}_{22}"] ["math" "{M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+{M}_{12}+{M}_{22}"] ["math" "{M}_{11}-{M}_{21}+{M}_{12}-{M}_{22}"] ["math" "{M}_{11}\\cdot {M}_{21}\\cdot {M}_{12}\\cdot {M}_{22}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/state.json" "file_sha256" "610429aa6dd5fcf5908c798c40fcb2ff3748ed1a5c18b759c6a6f7829018963a"} {"question" "q-122847" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2{M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+2{M}_{12}+{M}_{22}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/state.json" "file_sha256" "610429aa6dd5fcf5908c798c40fcb2ff3748ed1a5c18b759c6a6f7829018963a"} {"question" "q-123054" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/0b41aab14e66fe196a2720e65d4f814da55acfdb0c7b8338ff9e44acfe808785.png)\n\nConsider the partition of the rectangular region $R$ shown above and the positive function $f(x,y)$ defined on $R.$ Which of the following gives an expression for the upper Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to this partition.\n\n*Hint: ${M}_{ij}$ denotes the maximum value of $f$ on the subregion ${R}_{ij}$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/state.json" "file_sha256" "610429aa6dd5fcf5908c798c40fcb2ff3748ed1a5c18b759c6a6f7829018963a"} {"question" "q-123054" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To calculate the upper Riemann sum $U$ with respect to the given partition, we compute the sum of the volumes of $2$ rectangular solids, one for each ${R}_{ij}.$\n\nThe volume of the rectangular solid corresponding to each subregion ${R}_{ij}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{M}_{ij}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{ij}).\n$$\n\nwhere ${M}_{ij}$ denotes the maximum value of $f$ on the subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nSo in our case, we have\n\n$$\nU={M}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{M}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21}).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}({R}_{11})=2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}({R}_{21})=10.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =2\\cdot {M}_{11}+10\\cdot {M}_{21} \\\\  & =2{M}_{11}+10{M}_{21}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/state.json" "file_sha256" "610429aa6dd5fcf5908c798c40fcb2ff3748ed1a5c18b759c6a6f7829018963a"} {"question" "q-123054" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "30305aa75b06bac37b4cc4676cd685805b15f81588548b9edcb6adcf66fef700"} {"question" "q-123054" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/state.json" "file_sha256" "610429aa6dd5fcf5908c798c40fcb2ff3748ed1a5c18b759c6a6f7829018963a"} {"question" "q-123054" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "10{M}_{11}+2{M}_{21}"] ["math" "20{M}_{11}\\cdot {M}_{21}"] ["math" "2{M}_{11}+10{M}_{21}"] ["math" "{M}_{11}+5{M}_{21}"] ["math" "{M}_{11}+{M}_{21}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/state.json" "file_sha256" "610429aa6dd5fcf5908c798c40fcb2ff3748ed1a5c18b759c6a6f7829018963a"} {"question" "q-123054" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2{M}_{11}+10{M}_{21}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/state.json" "file_sha256" "610429aa6dd5fcf5908c798c40fcb2ff3748ed1a5c18b759c6a6f7829018963a"} {"question" "q-75232" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $R=[1,3]\\times [2,4]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=30-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={1,2,3},\\,{P}_{2}={2,3,4},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the upper Riemann sum $U(f,P).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/state.json" "file_sha256" "610429aa6dd5fcf5908c798c40fcb2ff3748ed1a5c18b759c6a6f7829018963a"} {"question" "q-75232" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/b7581f31a2031181aeb31bbdf8a3da6beff69b50ee41d5d73babbe198997fe15.png)\n\nThe upper sum $U(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={M}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{M}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{M}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{M}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${M}_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=1$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $U(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+{M}_{12}+{M}_{22}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=30-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=30-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/dfed66710976869aa4507aea2e171c09ac248d4b8b4ca083972dbd10952af6b7.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/a6366799e116eb35e8d3cb5401f0abc161ad183cd8f634bdaf2ce387f64d6c5c.png)\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{11} & =f(1,2)=25 \\\\ {M}_{21} & =f(2,2)=22 \\\\ {M}_{12} & =f(1,3)=20 \\\\ {M}_{22} & =f(2,3)=17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & =25+22+20+17 \\\\  & =84.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/state.json" "file_sha256" "610429aa6dd5fcf5908c798c40fcb2ff3748ed1a5c18b759c6a6f7829018963a"} {"question" "q-75232" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "30305aa75b06bac37b4cc4676cd685805b15f81588548b9edcb6adcf66fef700"} {"question" "q-75232" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/state.json" "file_sha256" "610429aa6dd5fcf5908c798c40fcb2ff3748ed1a5c18b759c6a6f7829018963a"} {"question" "q-75232" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "44"] ["math" "47"] ["math" "48"] ["math" "74"] ["math" "84"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/state.json" "file_sha256" "610429aa6dd5fcf5908c798c40fcb2ff3748ed1a5c18b759c6a6f7829018963a"} {"question" "q-75232" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "84"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/state.json" "file_sha256" "610429aa6dd5fcf5908c798c40fcb2ff3748ed1a5c18b759c6a6f7829018963a"} {"question" "q-75048" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $R=[-1,1]\\times [-1,1]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=5-3x-y.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={-1,0,1},\\,{P}_{2}={-1,0,1},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the upper Riemann sum $U(f,P).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/state.json" "file_sha256" "610429aa6dd5fcf5908c798c40fcb2ff3748ed1a5c18b759c6a6f7829018963a"} {"question" "q-75048" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/859d9d6b15dbd08b4c015816e46986268f92a94770df866a562f3e379bae5365.png)\n\nThe upper sum $U(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={M}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{M}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{M}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{M}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${M}_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=1$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $U(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+{M}_{12}+{M}_{22}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=5-3x-y$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=5-3x-y$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/4c688c7cf529800b8f1ad075dc70784e40a59163330031b375eaad4b1cdd64c9.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/6269ba3c4fd853fff89327ab9e6968e2f52439f5c86c0ec5a21dce1620d9ba5d.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{11} & =f(-1,-1)=9 \\\\ {M}_{21} & =f(0,-1)=6 \\\\ {M}_{12} & =f(-1,0)=8 \\\\ {M}_{22} & =f(0,0)=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & =9+6+8+5 \\\\  & =28.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/state.json" "file_sha256" "610429aa6dd5fcf5908c798c40fcb2ff3748ed1a5c18b759c6a6f7829018963a"} {"question" "q-75048" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "30305aa75b06bac37b4cc4676cd685805b15f81588548b9edcb6adcf66fef700"} {"question" "q-75048" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/state.json" "file_sha256" "610429aa6dd5fcf5908c798c40fcb2ff3748ed1a5c18b759c6a6f7829018963a"} {"question" "q-75048" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "20"] ["math" "24"] ["math" "28"] ["math" "30"] ["math" "34"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/state.json" "file_sha256" "610429aa6dd5fcf5908c798c40fcb2ff3748ed1a5c18b759c6a6f7829018963a"} {"question" "q-75048" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "28"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/state.json" "file_sha256" "610429aa6dd5fcf5908c798c40fcb2ff3748ed1a5c18b759c6a6f7829018963a"} {"question" "q-123235" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $R=[2,5]\\times [0,4]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=24-2x-3y.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={2,5},\\,{P}_{2}={0,1,2,3,4},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the upper Riemann sum $U(f,P).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/state.json" "file_sha256" "610429aa6dd5fcf5908c798c40fcb2ff3748ed1a5c18b759c6a6f7829018963a"} {"question" "q-123235" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/017b40453041b9485436e21f4966e530b925f6989795283e664692265ca2f814.png)\n\nThe upper sum $U(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={M}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{M}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{M}_{13}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{13})+{M}_{14}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{14}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${M}_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=3$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $U(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & =3\\cdot {M}_{11}+3\\cdot {M}_{12}+3\\cdot {M}_{13}+3\\cdot {M}_{14} \\\\  & =3({M}_{11}+{M}_{12}+{M}_{13}+{M}_{14}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=24-2x-3y$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=24-2x-3y$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/6ceecc26bb9800a2884202b05144dcecbf988bea7953605e4b33e485a4cb1865.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/f9ec123fe8048f8463c845527162a3f28ecc3a597f7f57717dde88909f363906.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{11} & =f(2,0)=20 \\\\ {M}_{12} & =f(2,1)=17 \\\\ {M}_{13} & =f(2,2)=14 \\\\ {M}_{14} & =f(2,3)=11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & =3(20+17+14+11) \\\\  & =186.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/state.json" "file_sha256" "610429aa6dd5fcf5908c798c40fcb2ff3748ed1a5c18b759c6a6f7829018963a"} {"question" "q-123235" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "30305aa75b06bac37b4cc4676cd685805b15f81588548b9edcb6adcf66fef700"} {"question" "q-123235" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/state.json" "file_sha256" "610429aa6dd5fcf5908c798c40fcb2ff3748ed1a5c18b759c6a6f7829018963a"} {"question" "q-123235" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "156"] ["math" "171"] ["math" "177"] ["math" "186"] ["math" "198"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/state.json" "file_sha256" "610429aa6dd5fcf5908c798c40fcb2ff3748ed1a5c18b759c6a6f7829018963a"} {"question" "q-123235" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "186"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/state.json" "file_sha256" "610429aa6dd5fcf5908c798c40fcb2ff3748ed1a5c18b759c6a6f7829018963a"} {"question" "q-123233" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $R=[0,1]\\times [0,3]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=8-4x-y.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={0,1},\\,{P}_{2}={0,1,2,3},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the upper Riemann sum $U(f,P).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/state.json" "file_sha256" "610429aa6dd5fcf5908c798c40fcb2ff3748ed1a5c18b759c6a6f7829018963a"} {"question" "q-123233" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/6fffcb4b3db9eab7d8bd7fbd253ed533ee95bc22eb5291b8b58041091e83b241.png)\n\nThe upper sum $U(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={M}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{M}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{M}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{13}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${M}_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=1$ for all $3$ subregions, the formula for $U(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={M}_{11}+{M}_{12}+{M}_{13}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=8-4x-y$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=8-4x-y$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/eda5e419fe95a7176f241c64f7d2452267fc2bc87d1e351b990e24665e9f0f94.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/cd1793250b5f95950acd195afc4d72ff94ac4eaee4719ad3e4064b8e9d5b8af6.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{11} & =f(0,0)=8 \\\\ {M}_{12} & =f(0,1)=7 \\\\ {M}_{13} & =f(0,2)=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & =8+7+6 \\\\  & =21.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/state.json" "file_sha256" "610429aa6dd5fcf5908c798c40fcb2ff3748ed1a5c18b759c6a6f7829018963a"} {"question" "q-123233" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "30305aa75b06bac37b4cc4676cd685805b15f81588548b9edcb6adcf66fef700"} {"question" "q-123233" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/state.json" "file_sha256" "610429aa6dd5fcf5908c798c40fcb2ff3748ed1a5c18b759c6a6f7829018963a"} {"question" "q-123233" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "18"] ["math" "19"] ["math" "20"] ["math" "21"] ["math" "22"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/state.json" "file_sha256" "610429aa6dd5fcf5908c798c40fcb2ff3748ed1a5c18b759c6a6f7829018963a"} {"question" "q-123233" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "21"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/state.json" "file_sha256" "610429aa6dd5fcf5908c798c40fcb2ff3748ed1a5c18b759c6a6f7829018963a"} {"question" "q-123238" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $R=[2,8]\\times [3,7]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=15-x-y.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={2,5,8},\\,{P}_{2}={3,5,7},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the upper Riemann sum $U(f,P).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/state.json" "file_sha256" "610429aa6dd5fcf5908c798c40fcb2ff3748ed1a5c18b759c6a6f7829018963a"} {"question" "q-123238" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/e216f0629c9213ab54c869e9eede5bf61b717c45aed872ad9791781bf7af180c.png)\n\nThe upper sum $U(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={M}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{M}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{M}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{M}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22})\n$$\n\nwhere ${M}_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=6$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $U(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & =6\\cdot {M}_{11}+6\\cdot {M}_{21}+6\\cdot {M}_{12}+6\\cdot {M}_{22} \\\\  & =6({M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+{M}_{12}+{M}_{22}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=15-x-y$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=15-x-y$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/a6249a828aebb94d1149f0083fee68e026b8c2ff1c32a3ae3b1c0471910a6c68.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/b162af4ffab30303889adc3ec1fae04e1a8cc943826972429643a71c2a2ed454.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{11} & =f(2,3)=10 \\\\ {M}_{21} & =f(5,3)=7 \\\\ {M}_{12} & =f(2,5)=8 \\\\ {M}_{22} & =f(5,5)=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & =6(10+7+8+5) \\\\  & =180.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/state.json" "file_sha256" "610429aa6dd5fcf5908c798c40fcb2ff3748ed1a5c18b759c6a6f7829018963a"} {"question" "q-123238" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "30305aa75b06bac37b4cc4676cd685805b15f81588548b9edcb6adcf66fef700"} {"question" "q-123238" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/state.json" "file_sha256" "610429aa6dd5fcf5908c798c40fcb2ff3748ed1a5c18b759c6a6f7829018963a"} {"question" "q-123238" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "120"] ["math" "180"] ["math" "30"] ["math" "60"] ["math" "90"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/state.json" "file_sha256" "610429aa6dd5fcf5908c798c40fcb2ff3748ed1a5c18b759c6a6f7829018963a"} {"question" "q-123238" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "180"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/state.json" "file_sha256" "610429aa6dd5fcf5908c798c40fcb2ff3748ed1a5c18b759c6a6f7829018963a"} {"question" "q-75049" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $R=[0,2]\\times [0,2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=x+y+1.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={0,1,2},\\,{P}_{2}={0,1,2},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the upper Riemann sum $U(f,P).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/state.json" "file_sha256" "610429aa6dd5fcf5908c798c40fcb2ff3748ed1a5c18b759c6a6f7829018963a"} {"question" "q-75049" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/3cac974f7352243bfbe008ad8af58ae8222709f81f434070383cc759a46747c7.png)\n\nThe upper sum $U(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={M}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{M}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{M}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{M}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${M}_{ij}$ is the maximum value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=1$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $U(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+{M}_{12}+{M}_{22}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=x+y+1$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=x+y+1$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/d7e548ae50bc8c7f0e33e8bb43ba8950788f735af103c4923d50d147995a3bb6.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *top right corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/6aee54ead228fa1f7cda200a25db0d745634dec8194d492d9075da82dde3ce5c.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{11} & =f(1,1)=3 \\\\ {M}_{21} & =f(2,1)=4 \\\\ {M}_{12} & =f(1,2)=4 \\\\ {M}_{22} & =f(2,2)=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & =3+4+4+5 \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/state.json" "file_sha256" "610429aa6dd5fcf5908c798c40fcb2ff3748ed1a5c18b759c6a6f7829018963a"} {"question" "q-75049" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "30305aa75b06bac37b4cc4676cd685805b15f81588548b9edcb6adcf66fef700"} {"question" "q-75049" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/state.json" "file_sha256" "610429aa6dd5fcf5908c798c40fcb2ff3748ed1a5c18b759c6a6f7829018963a"} {"question" "q-75049" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "11"] ["math" "14"] ["math" "16"] ["math" "7"] ["math" "9"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/state.json" "file_sha256" "610429aa6dd5fcf5908c798c40fcb2ff3748ed1a5c18b759c6a6f7829018963a"} {"question" "q-75049" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "16"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/state.json" "file_sha256" "610429aa6dd5fcf5908c798c40fcb2ff3748ed1a5c18b759c6a6f7829018963a"} {"question" "q-75182" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $R=[-2,2]\\times [-2,2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=2x+y+10.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={-2,0,2},\\,{P}_{2}={-2,0,2},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the upper Riemann sum $U(f,P).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/state.json" "file_sha256" "610429aa6dd5fcf5908c798c40fcb2ff3748ed1a5c18b759c6a6f7829018963a"} {"question" "q-75182" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/6a44295e07e8d14dfae78403d4629bde77c522136d5ffec16ace6416db72303e.png)\n\nThe upper sum $U(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={M}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{M}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{M}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{M}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${M}_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=4$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $U(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & =4\\cdot {M}_{11}+4\\cdot {M}_{21}+4\\cdot {M}_{12}+4\\cdot {M}_{22} \\\\  & =4({M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+{M}_{12}+{M}_{22}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=2x+y+10$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=2x+y+10$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/bdb4b6095c1b449d8e048f85a0d4d289758597af520ed61f545dbe280560ca15.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *top right corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/ca1c7e423afd88b0f1b18570bad73897092167ad338dcdbe275aaf451a08b3cb.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{11} & =f(0,0)=10 \\\\ {M}_{21} & =f(2,0)=14 \\\\ {M}_{12} & =f(0,2)=12 \\\\ {M}_{22} & =f(2,2)=16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & =4(10+14+12+16) \\\\  & =208.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/state.json" "file_sha256" "610429aa6dd5fcf5908c798c40fcb2ff3748ed1a5c18b759c6a6f7829018963a"} {"question" "q-75182" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "30305aa75b06bac37b4cc4676cd685805b15f81588548b9edcb6adcf66fef700"} {"question" "q-75182" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/state.json" "file_sha256" "610429aa6dd5fcf5908c798c40fcb2ff3748ed1a5c18b759c6a6f7829018963a"} {"question" "q-75182" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "120"] ["math" "196"] ["math" "208"] ["math" "52"] ["math" "88"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/state.json" "file_sha256" "610429aa6dd5fcf5908c798c40fcb2ff3748ed1a5c18b759c6a6f7829018963a"} {"question" "q-75182" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "208"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/state.json" "file_sha256" "610429aa6dd5fcf5908c798c40fcb2ff3748ed1a5c18b759c6a6f7829018963a"} {"question" "q-75186" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $R=[3,5]\\times [1,3]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}-2.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={3,4,5},\\,{P}_{2}={1,2,3},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the upper Riemann sum $U(f,P).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/state.json" "file_sha256" "610429aa6dd5fcf5908c798c40fcb2ff3748ed1a5c18b759c6a6f7829018963a"} {"question" "q-75186" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/ed8a998b543cb951a12599de55004c9220a53ac252a9eea66577475e2b8b6093.png)\n\nThe upper sum $U(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={M}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{M}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{M}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{M}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${M}_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=1$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $U(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+{M}_{12}+{M}_{22}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}-2$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}-2$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/a0033237f985416771125780a3d9b68bdfd8c6c33b9a4b0f2a32fa7af4c59bca.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *top right corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/26cae8b6e2c69c2c0c66d78eb64c5d02fc500d10a970e54b09f6ee72bc145b74.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{11} & =f(4,2)=26 \\\\ {M}_{21} & =f(5,2)=35 \\\\ {M}_{12} & =f(4,3)=41 \\\\ {M}_{22} & =f(5,3)=50\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & =26+35+41+50 \\\\  & =152.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/state.json" "file_sha256" "610429aa6dd5fcf5908c798c40fcb2ff3748ed1a5c18b759c6a6f7829018963a"} {"question" "q-75186" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "30305aa75b06bac37b4cc4676cd685805b15f81588548b9edcb6adcf66fef700"} {"question" "q-75186" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/state.json" "file_sha256" "610429aa6dd5fcf5908c798c40fcb2ff3748ed1a5c18b759c6a6f7829018963a"} {"question" "q-75186" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "125"] ["math" "140"] ["math" "152"] ["math" "512"] ["math" "91"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/state.json" "file_sha256" "610429aa6dd5fcf5908c798c40fcb2ff3748ed1a5c18b759c6a6f7829018963a"} {"question" "q-75186" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "152"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/state.json" "file_sha256" "610429aa6dd5fcf5908c798c40fcb2ff3748ed1a5c18b759c6a6f7829018963a"} {"question" "q-123132" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $R=[1,7]\\times [1,6]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=xy.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={1,3,5,7},\\,{P}_{2}={1,6},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the upper Riemann sum $U(f,P).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/state.json" "file_sha256" "610429aa6dd5fcf5908c798c40fcb2ff3748ed1a5c18b759c6a6f7829018963a"} {"question" "q-123132" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/68270610704b1ef132469193e7948f54e548fa22223b6fc49b693753f998f874.png)\n\nThe upper sum $U(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={M}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{M}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{M}_{31}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{31}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${M}_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=10$ for all $3$ subregions, the formula for $U(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & =10\\cdot {M}_{11}+10\\cdot {M}_{21}+10\\cdot {M}_{31} \\\\  & =10({M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+{M}_{31}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=xy$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=xy$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/7611c64cc460db725893156f6043644b34962a04f2aacef0b19cf99c5769cda3.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *top right corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/75f378700ce6593a7ed9577b34e093cbbfe702e9512e9962e0d6f7f681694842.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{11} & =f(3,6)=18 \\\\ {M}_{21} & =f(5,6)=30 \\\\ {M}_{31} & =f(7,6)=42\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & =10(18+30+42) \\\\  & =900.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/state.json" "file_sha256" "610429aa6dd5fcf5908c798c40fcb2ff3748ed1a5c18b759c6a6f7829018963a"} {"question" "q-123132" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "30305aa75b06bac37b4cc4676cd685805b15f81588548b9edcb6adcf66fef700"} {"question" "q-123132" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/state.json" "file_sha256" "610429aa6dd5fcf5908c798c40fcb2ff3748ed1a5c18b759c6a6f7829018963a"} {"question" "q-123132" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "320"] ["math" "45"] ["math" "450"] ["math" "90"] ["math" "900"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/state.json" "file_sha256" "610429aa6dd5fcf5908c798c40fcb2ff3748ed1a5c18b759c6a6f7829018963a"} {"question" "q-123132" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "900"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/state.json" "file_sha256" "610429aa6dd5fcf5908c798c40fcb2ff3748ed1a5c18b759c6a6f7829018963a"} {"question" "q-75175" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $R=[1,2]\\times [3,5]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=3x+2y.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={1,2},\\,{P}_{2}={3,4,5},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the upper Riemann sum $U(f,P).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/state.json" "file_sha256" "610429aa6dd5fcf5908c798c40fcb2ff3748ed1a5c18b759c6a6f7829018963a"} {"question" "q-75175" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/e51bb7736f316547ca179513705721f20ea2404a2713c7ae07af3a03616e7373.png)\n\nThe upper sum $U(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={M}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{M}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${M}_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ becuase we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=1$ for both subregions, the formula for $U(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={M}_{11}+{M}_{12}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=3x+2y$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=3x+2y$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/0aa78337b37fe81cbf4b3beba78967c92191f8ca7f9445931f5a4d532f806e53.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *top right corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/26fba6a927a7d7d097f278fbc2902e056ceb41356377b23f138a1598ac3d22dc.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{11} & =f(2,4)=14 \\\\ {M}_{12} & =f(2,5)=16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & =14+16 \\\\  & =30.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/state.json" "file_sha256" "610429aa6dd5fcf5908c798c40fcb2ff3748ed1a5c18b759c6a6f7829018963a"} {"question" "q-75175" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "30305aa75b06bac37b4cc4676cd685805b15f81588548b9edcb6adcf66fef700"} {"question" "q-75175" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/state.json" "file_sha256" "610429aa6dd5fcf5908c798c40fcb2ff3748ed1a5c18b759c6a6f7829018963a"} {"question" "q-75175" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "14"] ["math" "16"] ["math" "28"] ["math" "30"] ["math" "32"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/state.json" "file_sha256" "610429aa6dd5fcf5908c798c40fcb2ff3748ed1a5c18b759c6a6f7829018963a"} {"question" "q-75175" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "30"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/state.json" "file_sha256" "610429aa6dd5fcf5908c798c40fcb2ff3748ed1a5c18b759c6a6f7829018963a"} {"question" "e-6246" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/33269c79413f4435d074d90cd727f2a53e7b9831300bfcaf0b5a7122df764080.png)\n\nConsider the partition of the rectangular region $R$ shown above and the positive function $f(x,y)$ defined on $R.$ Find an expression for the upper Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to this partition.\n\n*Hint: $M_{ij}$ denotes the maximum value of $f$ on the subregion $R_{ij}$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/example-6246.json" "file_sha256" "19e126d91fee3378ebbd98918c75132bf140a140a93555e9e22490d3413fbed8"} {"question" "e-6246" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To calculate the upper Riemann sum $U$ with respect to the given partition, we compute the sum of the volumes of $2$ rectangular solids, one for each $R_{ij}.$\n\nThe volume of the rectangular solid corresponding to each subregion $R_{ij}$ is given by\n\n$$\nM_{ij}\\cdot \\textrm{Area}(R_{ij}).\n$$\n\nwhere $M_{ij}$ denotes the maximum value of $f$ on the subregion $R_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nSo in our case, we have\n\n$$\nU = M_{11}\\cdot \\textrm{Area}(R_{11}) + M_{21}\\cdot \\textrm{Area}(R_{21}).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(R_{11}) = 8\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(R_{21}) = 4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =8\\cdot {M}_{11}+4\\cdot {M}_{21} \\\\  & =8{M}_{11}+4{M}_{21}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/example-6246.json" "file_sha256" "19e126d91fee3378ebbd98918c75132bf140a140a93555e9e22490d3413fbed8"} {"question" "e-6245" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $R = [1,5] \\times [2,6]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y) = 65-x^2-y^2.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={1,3,5},\\,{P}_{2}={2,4,6},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the upper Riemann sum $U(f,P).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/example-6245.json" "file_sha256" "81137a912f5aeddda92e6df4fe8258138e54493d484bd231a0479eebc10afe7b"} {"question" "e-6245" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/5133b73c3707b87725baeee9a34a64898d33056dab9d7d32ac8cc24dbe772321.png)\n\nThe upper sum $U(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nU(f,P) = M_{11}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{11}) + M_{21}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{21}) + M_{12}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{12}) + M_{22}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{22}),\n$$\n\nwhere $M_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion $R_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\textrm{Area}(R_{ij})=4$ for all $4$ regions, the formula for $U(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & =4\\cdot {M}_{11}+4\\cdot {M}_{21}+4\\cdot {M}_{12}+4\\cdot {M}_{22} \\\\  & =4({M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+{M}_{12}+{M}_{22}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y) = 65-x^2-y^2$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y) = 65-x^2-y^2$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/5b711e2c33e8966dfc9f4274a8f556f712d51a450bbcd0cadb68771fd050d304.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/ad83cf465252b1f880757405ca3ff9ab5e1b9b1e50d712bd3b81305b7973af27.png)\n\nCalculating the value of $f$ at each bottom left corner, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{11} & =f(1,2)=60 \\\\ {M}_{21} & =f(3,2)=52 \\\\ {M}_{12} & =f(1,4)=48 \\\\ {M}_{22} & =f(3,4)=40\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above values in our expression for $U,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & =4(60+52+48+40)=800.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/example-6245.json" "file_sha256" "81137a912f5aeddda92e6df4fe8258138e54493d484bd231a0479eebc10afe7b"} {"question" "e-6244" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $R = [0,2]\\times [1,3]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y) = x+2y.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={0,1,2},\\,{P}_{2}={1,2,3},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the upper Riemann sum $U(f,P).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/example-6244.json" "file_sha256" "6c0e62534f2c52740b932982e76781c763df1ef5a58917bbb7e6b3cc7e9d8fe3"} {"question" "e-6244" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/4faf932e63a57d2fe53fb3942cfe21c65d0d2a88692c665b2706bd68164855c9.png)\n\nThe upper sum $U(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nU(f,P) = M_{11}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{11}) + M_{21}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{21}) + M_{12}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{12}) + M_{22}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{22}),\n$$\n\nwhere $M_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion $R_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum,\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\textrm{Area}(R_{ij})=1$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $U(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nU(f,P) = M_{11} + M_{21} + M_{12} + M_{22}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y) = x+2y$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y) = x+2y$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/26dc2a5af4c82657d9ec6fa331d4f5bc7575367bd4e3a00a5929b4ecaffe4477.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y$. This is the value at the *top right corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/6d34737c12c35f6faec5caf37be500ba16c3f7cb18bf5ae660dc6d54182b66da.png)\n\nCalculating the value of $f$ at each top right corner, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{11} & =f(1,2)=5 \\\\ {M}_{21} & =f(2,2)=6 \\\\ {M}_{12} & =f(1,3)=7 \\\\ {M}_{22} & =f(2,3)=8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above values in our expression for $U,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & =5+6+7+8=26.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/example-6244.json" "file_sha256" "6c0e62534f2c52740b932982e76781c763df1ef5a58917bbb7e6b3cc7e9d8fe3"}] "usage" {"q-75048" [] "e-6244" [] "q-122847" [] "e-6246" [] "e-6245" []} "basis" 2629 "retractions" [[17592186152320 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2614/Source/Images/q-122847.png)\n\nConsider the partition of the rectangular region $R$ shown above and the positive function $f(x, y)$ defined on $R$. Which of the following gives an expression for the upper Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to this partition.\n\n*Hint: $M_{ij}$ denotes the maximum value of $f$ on the subregion $R_{ij}$*"] [17592186152634 :question/problem "Let $R = [- 1, 1] \\cdot [- 1, 1]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x, y) = 5 - 3x - y$.\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R$, where\n\n$P_{1} = {-1, 0, 1}, P_{2} = {-1, 0, 1}, P = P_{1} \\cdot P_{2}$.\n\nCalculate the upper Riemann sum $U(f, P)$."] [17592186151865 :question/problem "Consider the partition of the rectangular region $R$ shown above and the positive function $f(x,y)$ defined on $R$. Find an expression for the upper Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to this partition.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2614/Source/Images/7bd8e396e842ee10967b4656ad76cdd5.png)\n\n*Hint: $M_{ij}$ denotes the maximum value of $f$ on the subregion $R_{ij}$*"] [17592186151865 :question/worked-solution "To calculate the upper Riemann sum $U$ with respect to the given partition, we compute the sum of the volumes of $2$ rectangular solids, one for each $R_{ij}$.\n\nThe volume of the rectangular solid corresponding to each subregion $R_{ij}$ is given by\n\n$$\nM_{ij}\\cdot \\textrm{Area}(R_{ij})\n$$\n\nwhere $M_{ij}$ denotes the maximum value of $f$ on the subregion $R_{ij}$. We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nSo in our case, we have\n\n$$\nU = M_{11}\\cdot \\textrm{Area}(R_{11}) + M_{21}\\cdot \\textrm{Area}(R_{21})\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(R_{11}) = 8\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(R_{21}) = 4\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nU &= 8 \\cdot M_{11} + 4 \\cdot M_{21} \\\\\n&= 8M_{11} + 4M_{21}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186151864 :question/problem "Let $R = [1,5] \\times [2,6]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y) = 65-x^2-y^2$.\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R$, where\n\n$P_{1} = {1, 3, 5}, P_{2} = {2, 4, 6}, P = P_{1} \\cdot P_{2}$.\n\nCalculate the upper Riemann sum $U(f,P)$."] [17592186151864 :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2614/Source/Images/9b2abc9b7e3a6bfcb6d7041a24fce9aa.png)\n\nThe upper sum $U(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nU(f,P) = M_{11}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{11}) + M_{21}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{21}) + M_{12}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{12}) + M_{22}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{22})\n$$\n\nwhere $M_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion $R_{ij}$. We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(R_{ij})=4\n$$\n\nfor all $4$ regions, the formula for $U(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nU(f, P) &= 4 \\cdot M_{11} + 4 \\cdot M_{21} + 4 \\cdot M_{12} + 4 \\cdot M_{22} \\\\\n&= 4(M_{11} + M_{21} + M_{12} + M_{22})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y) = 65-x^2-y^2$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n- $f(x,y) = 65-x^2-y^2$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2614/Source/Images/143b73cdc2be16101dddbb200c3c39b6.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y$. This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2614/Source/Images/09e5f4950e59ffc259e3ed3bef452a2f.png)\n\nCalculating the value of $f$ at each bottom left corner, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM_{11} &= f(1, 2) = 60 \\\\\nM_{21} &= f(3, 2) = 52 \\\\\nM_{12} &= f(1, 4) = 48 \\\\\nM_{22} &= f(3, 4) = 40\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above values in our expression for $U$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nU(f, P) &= 4(60 + 52 + 48 + 40) = 800\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186151863 :question/problem "Let $R = [0,2]\\times [1,3]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y) = x+2y$.\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R$, where\n\n$P_{1} = {0, 1, 2}, P_{2} = {1, 2, 3}, P = P_{1} \\cdot P_{2}$.\n\nCalculate the upper Riemann sum $U(f,P)$."] [17592186151863 :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2614/Source/Images/94f8ae5652f9bf448a84bd30a23dbe09.png)\n\nThe upper sum $U(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nU(f,P) = M_{11}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{11}) + M_{21}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{21}) + M_{12}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{12}) + M_{22}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{22})\n$$\n\nwhere $M_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion $R_{ij}$. We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum,\n\nTaking into account the fact that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(R_{ij})=1\n$$\n\nfor all $4$ subregions, the formula for $U(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nU(f,P) = M_{11} + M_{21} + M_{12} + M_{22}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y) = x+2y$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n- $f(x,y) = x+2y$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2614/Source/Images/c32290b459f231155ebed12f7dbb2413.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y$. This is the value at the *top right corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2614/Source/Images/e53b15ce6facb291ce49ed2b43c131db.png)\n\nCalculating the value of $f$ at each top right corner, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM_{11} &= f(1, 2) = 5 \\\\\nM_{21} &= f(2, 2) = 6 \\\\\nM_{12} &= f(1, 3) = 7 \\\\\nM_{22} &= f(2, 3) = 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above values in our expression for $U$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nU(f, P) &= 5 + 6 + 7 + 8 = 26\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186148855 17592186148856 17592186148857 17592186148858 17592186148859 17592186150715 17592186150716 17592186150717 17592186150718 17592186150719] "immutable_fields" [[17592186148854 :answer-field/correct] [17592186148854 :answer-field/id] [17592186148854 :answer-field/key] [17592186148854 :answer-field/type] [17592186150714 :answer-field/correct] [17592186150714 :answer-field/id] [17592186150714 :answer-field/key] [17592186150714 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
